<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<domain-models xmlns="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models
    http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models/domain-models_7.4.xsd">

  <module name="aiaccountant" package="com.axelor.apps.aiaccountant.db"/>

  <!-- What the server has measured about one chat panel's model conversation.

    WHY IT IS IN THE DATABASE. The figures that decide whether the retained conversation still fits
    inside the provider's context window used to travel only in the blob the browser stores and returns.
    That made a SAFETY decision depend on a value the caller supplies: a client that reported a smaller
    chain than it really had — by accident, by a stale bundle, or by editing the number — could keep a
    conversation chained past the point where the next request is refused, and the user loses the turn.
    Reporting a LARGER number is harmless (an early reset), so the exposure was one-directional and
    entirely in the wrong direction.

    So the measurements are recorded here, from the provider's own `usage` object, by the turn that
    produced them. The blob still carries them and they are still read — as a HINT, and only to notice
    that the two disagree, which is itself a reason to reset. Nothing the client sends can lower the
    number the decision is made on.

    SCOPE. One row per (user, company, conversation), the same key the workflow row uses and for the
    same reasons: a chain belongs to one person, in one set of books, in one chat panel. Two tabs are
    two chains and must never read each other's accounting.

    NOT AN AUTHORISATION. This row governs one thing — whether to keep chaining — and nothing else. It
    holds no target, no goal and no record reference, and no write is ever permitted or refused on the
    strength of it. What may be written is AiWorkflowState's question alone.

    LIFETIME. Written once per turn and read once per turn. Rows are small, bounded by the number of
    conversations a user opens, and are kept rather than deleted so a chain's growth history remains
    inspectable beside the turn metrics. -->
  <entity name="AiChatSession">

    <!-- `column` is explicit because `user` is an SQL keyword and the generator refuses to derive a
      column of that name. Same as AiWorkflowState, and stock does it wherever it holds a User. -->
    <many-to-one name="user" ref="com.axelor.auth.db.User" title="User" required="true"
      index="true" column="user_id"/>
    <many-to-one name="company" ref="com.axelor.apps.base.db.Company" title="Company"
      required="true" index="true"/>

    <!-- Which chat panel. Minted by the client, opaque here, only ever compared. See
      AiConversationKey — a value that fails its shape check never reaches this row. -->
    <string name="conversationId" title="Conversation" index="true"/>

    <!-- Which chain the measurements below describe. Minted whenever a turn starts a fresh model
      conversation, and never reused.

      WHY IT IS NEEDED. Two turns of one panel can be in flight at once — the user sends a second
      message while the first is still running, or the client retries. Without an identity for the
      chain, the turn that COMMITS last wins, and a turn that started earlier carries a smaller
      measurement: the row then reports a chain smaller than the real one, which is exactly the
      under-measurement that suppresses a required reset. It is the same hazard the client-side
      figures had, arriving through concurrency instead.

      HOW IT FENCES. A turn records which chain it was measuring. If the row has moved to a different
      chain since — somebody reset in between — the measurement describes a conversation that no
      longer exists and is discarded. Within one chain, `lastInputTokens` only ever grows (every
      request re-reads the whole retained history), so two concurrent turns are merged by keeping the
      larger, and an older one cannot pull the number down. -->
    <string name="chainId" title="Chain" index="true"/>

    <!-- Input tokens of the LAST model request of the last turn, as the provider reported them. This
      is the number every bound is checked against. -->
    <integer name="lastInputTokens" title="Last input tokens" default="0"/>

    <!-- How much the chain grew across that turn: its last request minus its first. What lets the next
      turn project its own peak instead of deciding from a measurement that is already one turn old. -->
    <integer name="chainGrowthTokens" title="Chain growth" default="0"/>

    <!-- How many turns this chain has run. A counter rather than a flag: it says whether a row that
      reports a small chain is a genuinely young conversation or one whose measurements stopped being
      written, and those two look identical without it. -->
    <integer name="turnCount" title="Turns" default="0"/>

    <!-- When the measurement was taken. A row nobody has updated for a long time cannot describe a
      chain the client is still sending an id for, so an old one is treated as no measurement at all. -->
    <datetime name="lastTurnDateTime" title="Last turn"/>

  </entity>

</domain-models>
